{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Rejoice.</b> Since all is vanity, rejoice now, young man, in your youth. Its meaning is like saying: do evil and you will see what comes upon you, like \"exult, O Philistia, over me\" — which, in another place, is \"exult and triumph, O Philistia,\" and the meaning is like \"upon Edom I cast my sandal\" — which is forceful and strong — \"exult now, O Philistia, and you will see what I will do to you.\" And likewise \"be silent and rejoice, O daughter of Edom, who dwells in the land of Uz.\" And thus the verse concludes: <b>but know that for all these</b> — and it turns back upon the rejoicing and the good of the heart. \"And walk in the ways of your heart and in the sight of your eyes\" — the meaning of \"good of the heart\" in our language has three aspects: first, that it becomes good of itself, like \"his heart grew glad\"; second, that the heart brings good to the owner of the heart, like \"may your heart make you good\"; and third, that a person makes his heart good, like \"they make their heart good.\" And the meaning of this verse is like \"and you shall not stray after your hearts and after your eyes.\""], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:29:14.137062703Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}